  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 415 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nA weak upper disturbance (MCV) left over from last night's\nconvection continues to gradually shear out/weaken as it slides\nNE into the Siouxland region. There has been just enough lift on\nthe tail end of this wave to spark some isolated to widely\nscattered showers over N zones. Another area of isolated\ndevelopment has been along/E of a weak convergent zone (subtle\nmixing boundary?) mainly E of Hwy 281 where atmosphere is quite\njuicy with temps in upper 80s and dew points in mid 70s. Hard to\nsee either area really taking off next few hours in terms of \ncoverage or intensity given what should be gradually increasing \nsubsidence as what's left of the aforementioned upper wave moves\neven further away. \n\nUpper pattern remains generally unchanged from 24-48 hours\nago...so expect at least scattered convection to develop all\nalong the Front Range this afternoon, then move E onto the\nPlains this evening and overnight. Recent CAMs (including 12Z\nHREF and 18Z HRRR) have been placing the highest chances and\nstrongest activity across KS - closer to the main baroclinic\nzone evident in sfc obs - running SW to NE across the state.\nThis boundary, along with overnight LLJ, should help focus one\nor two MCSs overnight and into early Sunday morning. Similar to\npast few nights, appears the most robust activity will tend to\nfavor areas W and SW of the local area, but still...lingering\nMUCAPE and system organization (additional MCVs?) should carry\nat least a low-end damaging wind threat into mainly our KS zones\nafter midnight tonight. Believe we're seeing the beginning of\nsuch MCS development over SE Colorado now. \n\nBesides perhaps some lingering showers in SE zones early in the\nmorning, the brunt of the daytime hours Sunday appear dry and\npleasant...besides the usual/seasonable humidity. Should see\ndecreasing clouds through the day and highs top out in a very\ncomfortable range in the upper 70s to mid 80s. Very nice way to\nend the weekend!\n\nUpper pattern looks to make a slight transition from mainly \nSW/zonal as it is weekend to mainly zonal/NW for at least the\nfirst half of next week. This will be in response to a deepening\nupper low around the Hudson Bay in Canada and moderate ridging\nagain asserting itself over the Desert SW. This will favor\nfrequent chances for storms as the Canadian low forces a cold \nfront into the region late Mon/Tue that then stalls out \nsomewhere over the Central Plains through midweek. Frequent \nweak waves interacting with this front...along with the upslope \nflow along the Front Range behind the front...should provide \nthe necessary lift for at least scattered storms each afternoon \nthat then roll E during the evening and overnight - perhaps\naided at times by a nocturnal low level jet. This is the BROAD\nidea of what the pattern should look like, but of course, the\n\"devil is in the details\".\n\nMonday daytime hours should be mainly dry for most, if not all,\nlocations as the primary area for development looks to be over\nnorth central Nebraska. Some of this activity could slide SE\ninto the local area during the late evening and overnight and\npose a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for strong to severe wind\ngusts (50-60 MPH). Tuesday looks to be the warmest day of the\nweek, but specifics will depend on timing and placement of the\ncold front. KS zones have the best chance to remain ahead of the\nfront through peak heating, and the latest forecast there is for\nvery warm highs in the upper 90s. Tuesday looks to feature the\nbest chances for late afternoon and evening development overhead\nalong the boundary, and the latest SPC Day 4 outlook implies\nSlight Risk level severe probabilities (level 2 of 5). This\nthreat will obviously be refined in the coming days.\n\nWednesday currently looks to be the coolest day of the week\nbehind the front and perhaps in quite a bit of lingering cloud\ncover. Ensembles generally indicate declining rain chances late\nnext week and gradually recovering temperatures - though\nconfidence on specifics this far out is quite low.",
